artifact.self_contained_problem:
In relativistic nucleus–nucleus collisions one observes an enhancement of strange-hadron production relative to scaled proton–proton baselines, and, in particular, an enhancement of multi-strange antibaryons (such as $\bar{\Lambda}$, $\bar{\Xi}^+$, $\bar{\Omega}^+$) whose relative yields follow a characteristic hierarchy increasing with strangeness content. The standard explanation invokes local color deconfinement: a quark–gluon plasma in which gluon fusion $gg \to s\bar{s}$ and a chemically equilibrated, flavor-symmetric partonic phase generate both the overall enhancement and the observed relative antibaryon ratios.

Construct, or prove the impossibility of constructing, a dynamical model of normal confined hadronic matter (i.e., a system whose degrees of freedom are color-singlet hadrons, with no local deconfinement) that simultaneously satisfies the following requirements:

(a) it reproduces the experimentally measured strangeness enhancement factors in central A+A collisions at SPS, RHIC, and LHC energies, as functions of centrality and collision energy;

(b) it reproduces the observed pattern of relative yields of strange antibaryons $\bar{\Lambda},\bar{\Xi}^+,\bar{\Omega}^+$, including their hierarchy with strangeness number and their ratios to the corresponding baryons, consistent with the constraints imposed by detailed balance in purely hadronic reaction networks;

(c) it predicts at least one further observable consequence — distinct from strangeness yields — that would serve as an independent experimental signature of the proposed confined-matter mechanism and distinguish it from a deconfined quark–gluon plasma scenario.

Either exhibit such a model with explicit predictions for (a)–(c), or establish a no-go result showing that no model of confined hadronic matter consistent with detailed balance can satisfy (a) and (b) simultaneously.
